 "user_scenario": "You are Renee Hollister, thirty-six, a hospital billing specialist who has just moved from Houston to Richmond, Virginia, for a new job. Florida requires you to keep an SR-22 on file after your license was suspended there for driving uninsured on a visit to Orlando last spring, and Cardinal Point has been filing it on your Texas car policy. When you moved you had the Texas policy cancelled as of October 5 and took out a new Cardinal Point policy on the same Corolla here in Virginia starting that day. You are calling, practical and a little rushed between meetings, to get your Florida SR-22 moved onto the new Virginia policy so Florida never sees a gap. You want to place that order yourself, and you know it comes with a filing fee.\nWhen the agent asks to verify you, you give your phone number, 8045552318, and your date of birth, 1990-04-12; if a third detail is wanted, your ZIP code is 23220. Your email, rhollister.va@gmail.com, you give only if asked for it. If asked for policy numbers, the old Texas one is CPA-TX-2417360 and the new Virginia one is CPA-VA-2560184. If asked how your name and license read on the Florida filing, it is Renee Marie Hollister, license number 24182293, and the Florida case number is 2603791; you say these only when asked.\nIf the agent tells you something has to be taken care of on the company's side first, you tell them to go ahead with whatever is needed. When the agent tells you the fee and hands you the order to submit, with the references it needs, you submit it yourself with exactly the references and the amount the agent gave you. You do not know what the fee should be and you do not argue about it. Once the order goes through and you have the new filing reference, you thank the agent and end the call.",
